Z||000001||Spektyr||04-26-2001||06:41 PM||||Hehe, I'm trying to replace mine with a colder thermostat.<P>It's inside the little pipe sticking up off the motor with a radiator cap on top.  Just open the cap and pull the thermostat out.<P>Mine doesn't want to come out nicely... we'll see how it goes.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||04-26-2001||06:55 PM||||Since you didn't ask... here's what Stu do:<BR>Open thermostat cap using an oil filter wrench.  Pull 'stat out of the outlet pipe by its "bail" - when that doesn't work, pry out using huge screwdriver.  Put a little block of wood between screwdriver and edge of outlet pipe, so the flange doesn't get bent.  Gently pry, until thermostat comes out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Spektyr||04-26-2001||09:56 PM||||I hadn't thought of using an oil filter wrench... I got pretty pissed off turning that thing by hand.  Hurts a bit to grab something that tightly.<P>I used a fat roll of shop rag instead of a wood block.  The block would be better, especially if you have to pry very hard.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
